/* data/learn-c/mth-12-06.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/mth-12-06.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-06"] = {"6.1":{"s":"A line in space is fixed by a point on it and a direction, or by two points. In vector form every point of the line is r = a + λb, where a is the position vector of a known point, b is parallel to the line and λ runs over all reals. In Cartesian form the same line is written with the point's coordinates and the direction ratios a, b, c in a symmetric fraction form.","f":[{"x":"r = a + lambda*b","n":"Vector equation: through point a, parallel to vector b (vectors in bold in the book).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.195"},{"x":"(x - x_1)/a = (y - y_1)/b = (z - z_1)/c","n":"Cartesian (symmetric) form; a, b, c = direction ratios.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.195"},{"x":"r = a + lambda*(b - a)","n":"Through two points with position vectors a and b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.196"},{"x":"(x - x_1)/(x_2 - x_1) = (y - y_1)/(y_2 - y_1) = (z - z_1)/(z_2 - z_1)","n":"Cartesian form through two points.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.196"}],"w":["A line in space has no single Cartesian equation; it needs the pair of equations in the symmetric form."],"x":["A line in space is fixed by a point on it and its direction. Let the point A have position vector a, and let the line be parallel to the vector b. Any point P of the line has the position vector r = a + λb, where λ is a real number (a parameter). This is the vector equation of the line. As λ changes, the point P slides along the line.","In coordinates, write a = (x₁, y₁, z₁) and b = (l, m, n). Then x = x₁ + λl, y = y₁ + λm, z = z₁ + λn (the parametric form). Eliminating λ gives the Cartesian equation (x − x₁)/l = (y − y₁)/m = (z − z₁)/n. The numbers l, m, n are the direction ratios. If a denominator is zero, the numerator is zero too.","The direction cosines are the direction ratios divided by √(l² + m² + n²), and l² + m² + n² = 1 for them. The line through two points A and B has the direction AB = b − a, so r = a + λ(b − a).","The angle between two lines with direction vectors b₁ and b₂ is the acute angle θ with cosθ = |b₁ · b₂|/(|b₁||b₂|). They are perpendicular when b₁ · b₂ = 0, which means l₁l₂ + m₁m₂ + n₁n₂ = 0, and parallel when the direction ratios are in proportion."],"e":"The line through (1, 2, 3) with direction (2, −1, 4): (x − 1)/2 = (y − 2)/(−1) = (z − 3)/4. Its point for λ = 1 is (3, 1, 7)."},"6.2":{"s":"The perpendicular distance of a point P from a line r = a + λb comes from a right-angled triangle: the hypotenuse is AP, and one side is the projection of AP on the line's direction. Alternatively, write the foot of the perpendicular M in terms of λ, use the fact that PM is perpendicular to the line to find λ, then find PM.","f":[{"x":"d = sqrt(|alpha - a|^2 - ((alpha - a).b/|b|)^2)","n":"Distance of the point with position vector alpha from r = a + lambda b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.201"}],"w":["The distance of a point from a line is the perpendicular distance. Do not use the length of AP itself.","Use the component of AP perpendicular to the direction, which needs the cross product or Pythagoras."],"x":["The perpendicular distance of a point P from a line is the length of the shortest segment from P to the line. It is found by finding the foot of the perpendicular, or by a formula with the cross product.","Formula: let the line be r = a + λb and the point P have the position vector p. The vector AP = p − a. The distance is d = |(p − a) × b|/|b|. This works since |AP × b| = |AP||b| sinθ, so dividing by |b| gives |AP| sinθ, the perpendicular height.","To find the foot of the perpendicular, write a general point on the line, Q = a + λb, and use the condition that PQ is perpendicular to b: (Q − p) · b = 0. This gives λ, and so Q. The distance is |PQ|. The image of P in the line is found by using Q as the mid-point.","The same cross-product formula gives the distance between two parallel lines, r = a₁ + λb and r = a₂ + μb: d = |(a₂ − a₁) × b|/|b|, as it is the distance from a point of one line to the other line."],"e":"The distance of P(1, 0, 0) from the line through the origin with direction (1, 2, 2): (1, 0, 0) × (1, 2, 2) = (0, −2, 2), magnitude 2√2. Divided by |b| = 3, d = 0.943."},"6.3":{"s":"Skew lines are lines in space that neither meet nor are parallel. The shortest distance between them is along the segment perpendicular to both, which lies along b₁ × b₂. Two lines meet exactly when this distance is zero. For parallel lines the distance is found from the cross product of the joining vector with the unit direction vector.","f":[{"x":"d = |(a_2 - a_1).(b_1 x b_2)|/|b_1 x b_2|","n":"Shortest distance between skew lines r = a_1 + lambda b_1 and r = a_2 + mu b_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.203"},{"x":"d = |(a_2 - a_1) x b|/|b|","n":"Distance between parallel lines r = a_1 + lambda b and r = a_2 + mu b (the book uses the unit vector of b).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.205"}],"w":["If (a₂ − a₁)·(b₁ × b₂) = 0 the lines intersect (or are parallel); the skew-line formula then gives 0."],"x":["Two lines in a plane either meet or are parallel. In three dimensions, there is a third case: skew lines, which are neither parallel nor intersecting. They lie in different planes. Examples: a line along the edge of the floor of a room and one on the ceiling running in a different direction.","The shortest distance between two skew lines is the length of the common perpendicular, which is perpendicular to both. Its direction is that of b₁ × b₂. For the lines r = a₁ + λb₁ and r = a₂ + μb₂, the shortest distance is d = |(a₂ − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂)|/|b₁ × b₂|. The numerator is the volume of the parallelepiped on the three vectors, and the denominator the area of its base, so the quotient is the height.","If the shortest distance is zero, then (a₂ − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂) = 0, so the three vectors are coplanar, and the lines intersect (or are parallel). This is the condition for two lines to be coplanar. In Cartesian form it is a determinant with the rows (x₂ − x₁, y₂ − y₁, z₂ − z₁), (l₁, m₁, n₁) and (l₂, m₂, n₂).","For parallel lines, b₁ × b₂ = 0 and the formula above cannot be used. Use the formula of the previous topic."],"e":"Lines r = (1, 2, 3) + λ(1, −1, 1) and r = (2, 4, 5) + μ(2, 1, 1): b₁ × b₂ = (−2, 1, 3), (a₂ − a₁) = (1, 2, 2). The distance is |−2 + 2 + 6|/√14 = 6/√14 = 1.604."},"6.4":{"s":"A plane is fixed by a point and a normal vector, by a point and two vectors parallel to it, by three non-collinear points, or by its distance from the origin and its unit normal. All of these lead to the scalar product form r·n = d, whose Cartesian form is ax + by + cz = d with a, b, c the direction ratios of the normal. Planes through the line of intersection of two planes form a family.","f":[{"x":"r.n = a.n","n":"Plane through point a perpendicular to n; with a.n = d it is r.n = d.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.209"},{"x":"r.n_hat = p","n":"Normal form: p = distance from the origin, n_hat = unit normal; Cartesian lx + my + nz = p.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.213"},{"x":"r.(n_1 + lambda*n_2) = d_1 + lambda*d_2","n":"Planes through the line of intersection of r.n_1 = d_1 and r.n_2 = d_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.215"}],"w":["In the normal form p must be positive and n̂ a unit vector; divide by |n| first."],"x":["A plane is determined by a point on it and a vector normal to it. If the normal is n = (a, b, c) and the plane passes through P₀ with position vector r₀, a point r is on the plane if (r − r₀) · n = 0, which is r · n = r₀ · n = d. This is the vector equation of the plane. In coordinates, ax + by + cz = d, where (a, b, c) are the direction ratios of the normal.","Forms of the equation: through (x₁, y₁, z₁) with normal (a, b, c): a(x − x₁) + b(y − y₁) + c(z − z₁) = 0. Intercept form: x/p + y/q + z/r = 1, for the intercepts p, q and r on the axes. Normal form: r · n̂ = p, where n̂ is a unit normal and p is the perpendicular distance from the origin.","Plane through three non-collinear points A, B, C: (r − a) · [(b − a) × (c − a)] = 0, since (b − a) × (c − a) is a normal. In coordinates this is a determinant equal to zero. A plane through two points A and B and parallel to a vector d has the normal (b − a) × d.","Special planes: x = 0 is the yz-plane, y = 0 the zx-plane and z = 0 the xy-plane. A plane such as x = 5 is parallel to the yz-plane. The plane through the intersection of two planes P₁ = 0 and P₂ = 0 is P₁ + λP₂ = 0."],"e":"The plane through (1, 2, 3) with normal (2, −1, 2): 2(x − 1) − (y − 2) + 2(z − 3) = 0, which is 2x − y + 2z = 6."},"6.5":{"s":"The angle between two planes is the acute angle between their normals; they are perpendicular when the dot product of the normals is zero. The angle between a line and a plane is the complement of the angle between the line and the plane's normal, so its sine (not cosine) uses the dot product. A line is perpendicular to a plane when its direction is parallel to the normal.","f":[{"x":"cos(theta) = |n_1.n_2|/(|n_1|*|n_2|)","n":"Angle between planes r.n_1 = d_1 and r.n_2 = d_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.217"},{"x":"sin(theta) = |b.n|/(|b|*|n|)","n":"Angle between the line r = a + lambda b and the plane r.n = d.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.217"}],"w":["Line and plane: SINE of the angle; two planes or two lines: COSINE."],"x":["The angle between two planes is defined as the angle between their normals. For the planes a₁x + b₁y + c₁z + d₁ = 0 and a₂x + b₂y + c₂z + d₂ = 0, cosθ = |a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ + c₁c₂|/(√(a₁² + b₁² + c₁²) √(a₂² + b₂² + c₂²)). The modulus gives the acute angle.","Special cases: the planes are perpendicular if a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ + c₁c₂ = 0, and parallel if the normals are proportional, a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ = c₁/c₂. Parallel planes can be written with the same normal, ax + by + cz + d₁ = 0 and ax + by + cz + d₂ = 0, and the distance between them is |d₁ − d₂|/√(a² + b² + c²).","Two planes that are not parallel meet in a line. The line has the direction n₁ × n₂, perpendicular to both normals. A plane is also needed to describe a line (as the intersection of two planes).","The angle between a line (direction b) and a plane (normal n) is θ, where sinθ = |b · n|/(|b||n|), since the angle with the normal is 90° − θ. The line is parallel to the plane if b · n = 0, and perpendicular to it if b is parallel to n."],"e":"The planes x + y + z = 1 and x − y + z = 2 have normals (1, 1, 1) and (1, −1, 1): cosθ = 1/3, so θ = 70.53°."},"6.6":{"s":"Two lines are coplanar when they lie in one plane. Parallel lines always are. Non-parallel lines are coplanar exactly when the shortest distance between them is zero, that is, when the scalar triple product of the joining vector and the two directions vanishes. The plane holding them then passes through a point of either line with normal b₁ × b₂.","f":[{"x":"(a_2 - a_1).(b_1 x b_2) = 0","n":"Condition for the lines r = a_1 + lambda b_1 and r = a_2 + mu b_2 to be coplanar.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.218"},{"x":"(r - a_1).(b_1 x b_2) = 0","n":"Equation of the plane containing them.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.218"}],"w":["Parallel lines are always coplanar. Do not claim they are skew.","Non-parallel lines are coplanar only when the scalar triple product is zero; then they meet."],"x":["Two lines are coplanar if they lie in one plane. That happens when they are parallel, or when they intersect. Two skew lines are not coplanar.","For the lines r = a₁ + λb₁ and r = a₂ + μb₂, coplanarity means that the three vectors a₂ − a₁, b₁ and b₂ lie in one plane, so their scalar triple product is zero: (a₂ − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂) = 0. In coordinates, it is the determinant with rows (x₂ − x₁, y₂ − y₁, z₂ − z₁), (l₁, m₁, n₁), (l₂, m₂, n₂) equal to zero.","If the lines are coplanar, the plane that contains them has the normal b₁ × b₂ and passes through a₁: (r − a₁) · (b₁ × b₂) = 0. The point of intersection is found by solving x₁ + λl₁ = x₂ + μl₂, y₁ + λm₁ = y₂ + μm₂ and z₁ + λn₁ = z₂ + μn₂ for λ and μ (any two equations give them, and the third checks).","A typical exam problem gives two lines with an unknown constant k and asks for the value of k that makes them coplanar. Expand the determinant, set it to zero, and solve."],"e":"L₁ through (0, 0, 0) with direction (1, 2, 3), and L₂ through (1, 2, 3) with direction (3, 2, 1). Here a₂ − a₁ = (1, 2, 3) and b₁ × b₂ = (−4, 8, −4), and the dot product is −4 + 16 − 12 = 0, so the lines are coplanar. They meet at (1, 2, 3), for λ = 1 and μ = 0."},"6.7":{"s":"To find the distance of a point A from a plane, write the plane in normal form r·n̂ = p. The parallel plane through A is at distance a·n̂ from the origin, so the required distance is the difference of the two. In Cartesian form this gives the familiar formula with the absolute value of the plane's expression at the point, divided by the length of the normal.","f":[{"x":"d = |p - a.n_hat|","n":"Distance of the point a from the plane r.n_hat = p (plane in normal form).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.220"}],"w":["Convert the plane to normal form first; otherwise divide by |n|."],"x":["The perpendicular distance of a point P(x₁, y₁, z₁) from the plane ax + by + cz + d = 0 is |ax₁ + by₁ + cz₁ + d|/√(a² + b² + c²). Substituting the point into the left-hand side and dividing by the length of the normal gives the signed distance. In vector form, for the plane r · n = d, the distance of a point with position vector p is |p · n − d|/|n|.","Special cases: the distance of the origin is |d|/√(a² + b² + c²). Two points are on the same side of the plane if the expressions ax + by + cz + d have the same sign for them, and on opposite sides if the signs differ.","The foot of the perpendicular from P to the plane is P − [(ax₁ + by₁ + cz₁ + d)/(a² + b² + c²)](a, b, c). The image of P in the plane is found by going twice as far.","The distance between two parallel planes ax + by + cz + d₁ = 0 and ax + by + cz + d₂ = 0 is |d₁ − d₂|/√(a² + b² + c²), which is the distance from a point on one to the other."],"e":"The distance of (1, 2, 3) from 2x − 2y + z = 3: |2 − 4 + 3 − 3|/√9 = 2/3. The distance of the origin from 3x + 4y + 12z = 26 is 26/13 = 2."}};
